1	/// fs.rs — Read-only filesystem tools (Tier 0, Phase 1 / M1.2).
2	///
3	/// Implements `fs.read`, `fs.list`, `fs.stat` with TWO layers of defense:
4	///
5	/// 1. Pure `validate()` rejects obvious abuse (NUL bytes, percent-encoding,
6	///    backslashes) and enforces that the path is absolute and falls under a
7	///    whitelist root. Cross-platform, no IO, easy to fuzz.
8	/// 2. `openat2()` with `RESOLVE_BENEATH | RESOLVE_NO_SYMLINKS` atomically
9	///    opens within the whitelisted root. The kernel rejects any resolution
10	///    that traverses a symlink or escapes the root via `..`, so we are TOCTOU-
11	///    safe (the check and the open are one syscall).
12	///
13	/// Whitelist roots:
14	///   $HOME           — user's home directory (read access)
15	///   /proc, /sys     — kernel-exposed read-only data
16	///   /tmp            — temp scratch
17	///   /var/log        — system logs (read-only here)
18	///   /etc            — system config (read-only here)
19	///
20	/// Phase 1 reads are UTF-8 only with a 10 MB ceiling. Binary reads land in
21	/// Phase 5 alongside the base64 encoding layer. Writes (`fs.write` /
22	/// `fs.delete`) land in M1.5 with the COW gate (INV-6).
23	///
24	/// macOS dev fallback: `openat2` is Linux-only, so on macOS we fall back to
25	/// `std::fs::canonicalize` + prefix check. That fallback is for the dev loop
26	/// only — production runs on Linux with the openat2 path active.
